WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.920
We’ve located the roots of the zombi in&nbsp;

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.360
spiritual beliefs developed during&nbsp;
from the African diaspora, looked at

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.320
the Haitian zombi/pop culture zombie dichotomy,&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.360
and the role slavery played in how the monster was&nbsp;
shaped—and why it took hold in popular culture.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.360
Now we can explore one of the most influential&nbsp;
and enduring horror legacies of all time—

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.640
the Romero zombie.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.920
Pause with music up and animation

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.200
You simply can’t talk about&nbsp;
zombies without talking about...&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.240
….George A. Romero. Spanning 41 years,&nbsp;
his six-part zombie series (plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.840
two remakes!) introduced the&nbsp;
world to an evolving vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.720
and version of the monsters, starting&nbsp;
in 1968 with Night of the Living Dead.&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.640
Slow moving, flesh-eating, and very clearly&nbsp;
undead, the zombies presented in this first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.880
film impacted horror movies in ways that still&nbsp;
resonate today, and absolutely contributed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.840
Romero’s reputation as one of the most&nbsp;
significant directors of the genre.&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.200
More than just the reinvention of&nbsp;
a frightening fiend, the Romero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.400
zombie’s introduction during a time of great&nbsp;
political and cultural unrest&nbsp; in America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.160
impacted how it was received—and&nbsp;
why we still talk about it.&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.000
Whether it’s what he intended or not, Romero’s&nbsp;
work forever changed the way we look at zombies.&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.120
[Monstrum intro.]
Before we get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.000
the details of the film that put Romero on&nbsp;
the map, let’s talk about why his specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.480
portrayal of zombies is so notorious that&nbsp;
they get their own title (and video!).

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.080
After all, Romero wasn’t the first person&nbsp;
to show undead bodies eating living human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.000
flesh. But you could call him the Bram Stoker&nbsp;
of zombies—he took bits and pieces of existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.880
lore and mythology and combined them together&nbsp;
into one iconic monster for the first time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.720
Romero zombies are reanimated corpses&nbsp;
that crave human flesh, and literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.480
anyone can become one. They are almost always&nbsp;
slow-moving and in some cases can even use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.960
tools. They can be destroyed by a shot to the head&nbsp;
or a heavy blow to the skull. No clear explanation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.640
is ever given for why they returned from the&nbsp;
grave and unlike the Haitian zombi, Romero’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.640
creations are autonomous and even sometimes&nbsp;
self-aware; they answer to no human master.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.960
We can pretty confidently say that&nbsp;
Romero’s zombie looked so different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.080
because he wasn’t really aware&nbsp;
of the Haitian zombi tradition.&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.880
Just ask Daniel Kraus, the author who&nbsp;
finished Romero’s novel The Living Dead.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.200
I'm not convinced he knew much about Haitian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.280
zombies. He wasn't even thinking&nbsp;
about these as zombies at all.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.240
But he was just trying to create something&nbsp;
that would, that would be the chaos outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:44.933
the farmhouse.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:50.400
Um, so I don't think that shift really existed&nbsp;
in his mind. I don't know that the concept of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:57.520
the Haitian zombie was well enough known in America&nbsp;
for there to be that conscious of a shift.&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:07.680
Romero claims that it was another type of undead&nbsp;
creature that inspired him—the vampires in&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.800
Richard Matheson’s I Am Legend, a&nbsp;
book about a city ravaged by a virus.&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.360
In Night of the Living Dead, what we would&nbsp;
now identify as a zombie is actually called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.320
a “ghoul.” Romero eventually called the monsters&nbsp;
zombies, only because other people&nbsp; did.&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.680
I think some of it happened kind of, especially&nbsp;
with George Romero happened sort of organically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.240
And it's almost like after the fact&nbsp;
once they started being called zombies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.880
which would have been sometime, I&nbsp;
guess, early seventies or something,&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.600
certainly by Dawn of the Dead&nbsp;
they're calling them zombies.&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.960
Night of the Living Dead, is one of the most&nbsp;
culturally and critically acclaimed contributions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.320
to the zombie canon. The film follows a group&nbsp;
of survivors sheltering in a farmhouse in rural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.840
Pennsylvania after escaping from an attack by&nbsp;
suddenly-and-inexplicably-resurrected corpses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.800
The movie is centered around the characters&nbsp;
of Barbara, a woman in a catatonic state as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.720
a result of seeing her brother killed&nbsp;
and another woman half-eaten, and&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.160
Ben, the competent, resourceful&nbsp;
man who tries to save her.&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.000
Tension builds as the two leads&nbsp;
find themselves at odds with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.480
competing personalities and interests of&nbsp;
the other survivors sheltering with them.&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.000
Sounds like a&nbsp; traditional&nbsp;
set-up for a horror film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.720
right? So why did this film have&nbsp;
so much impact and staying power?

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.760
To get more insight into the Romero zombie,&nbsp;
I had a conversation with Tananarive Due,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.520
a screenwriter, author, and&nbsp;
executive producer of Horror Noire.&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.040
This film conceived and shot and the tumultuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.640
1960s against the backdrop&nbsp;
of the civil rights era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:52.400
which much like our contemporary era had really&nbsp;
disturbing images coming across people's screens.&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:58.400
So I don't think it's a surprise that&nbsp;
the 1960s in particular produced the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.000
violent zombies that would actually&nbsp;
invade your home. It's almost as if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.200
the violence on your television screen has&nbsp;
been brought to life and is trying to kill you.&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.880
he was shooting night, living dead, um,&nbsp;
in the style of news footage and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.560
conscious. Um, and he was basing it off of footage&nbsp;
and photographs of protesters being beat up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.880
that was conscious. So all these, you know,&nbsp;
all these decisions prove who the man is.&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.280
Emily from Tananarive Due Interview:
For me, the two scenes that stuck out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.360
the most were Ben being shot and&nbsp;
the little girl eating her mother,

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.960
I interpret that as also&nbsp;
sort of an uprising of youth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.753
culture and like a very violent visceral way.

00:05:40.753 --> 00:05:44.591
You know, we see this child devouring,&nbsp;
her mother represents it is just the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.591 --> 00:05:52.567
turned upside down. And as a kid watching&nbsp;
that, it's really horrifying because, uh,&nbsp;
you're engaging with the question of&nbsp;

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:59.865
how much violence is a child capable of and,&nbsp;&nbsp;
and, and this idea of being parentless,&nbsp;
being orphan.

00:05:59.865 --> 00:06:08.080
The final scene of the movie depicts Ben, the&nbsp;lone survivor among the farmhouse’s inhabitants,&nbsp;
and also the lone Black man in the entire film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.840
being shot in the head by a member&nbsp;
of an all-white rescue squad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.440
The town sheriff gives the order when he&nbsp;
mistakes Ben for one of the reanimated dead.&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:24.080
The rescuers who basically look like a Lynch&nbsp;
mob to not look like saviors to me as a black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.040
person. And sure enough, Duane Jones is shot&nbsp;
without even a word without even thought.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:39.360
...His history is erased. His heroism erased.&nbsp;
Just had to really really stun a lot of audiences.&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.160
Now, Romero consistently refuted that he meant to&nbsp;
make any connection between the murder of Ben and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:52.160
the Civil Rights movement sweeping America at&nbsp;
the time. He insisted that he cast Duane Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:56.000
the only black actor in the film,&nbsp;
because he had had the best audition.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:04.240
Even if you weren't thinking about racial strife&nbsp;
and the power difference between whites and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.920
blacks, when you cast Duane Jones, I have to think&nbsp;
that when he was looking at the daily footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:17.120
he had to be struck by the image of this dark&nbsp;
skin, black man taking charge throughout this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:23.200
movie, uh, punching Mr. Cooper, who definitely&nbsp;
represents the white patriarchy of the day

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:24.880
In 1960s America it seems impossible that&nbsp;
this moment would not have been seen as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:34.960
statement about Black power.&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.960
I don't think there were any black&nbsp;
zombies in Night of the Living Dead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.840
So he is killing white person after white person&nbsp;
after white person. I don't understand how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:50.720
can be looking at that footage and not realize,&nbsp;
wow, this is really going to hit people hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:55.280
this is going to hit people where it hurts.
The years preceding the release of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:59.520
itself were politically and socially&nbsp;
charged with conversations about race.&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:05.360
Brown v. Board of Education, declaring racial&nbsp;
segregation of schools unconstitutional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.000
had been passed only 14 years before.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:12.640
Ten years after Brown v. Board and only four&nbsp;
years before Romero’s film was released,&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:17.360
the Civil Rights Act of 1964 had&nbsp;
outlawed discrimination based on sex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.000
nationality, religion, color, and race.&nbsp;

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:25.600
Loving v. Virginia had given interracial&nbsp;
couples the legal right to wed in 1967&nbsp;

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:30.960
and that same year Star Trek featured the&nbsp;
first interracial kiss on television.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:35.760
Of course Romero, who shot the film in&nbsp;
1967, could not have predicted that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.800
assassination of Martin Luther King, Jr. would&nbsp;
occur the same year the movie was released.&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:44.560
This is Dr. Robin Means Coleman,&nbsp;
professor and author of Horror Noire.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:50.400
It's 1968, he's driving to New York,&nbsp;
he's got these film cans in the car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.040
in the trunk of the car, and he hears it.&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.000
Dr. Martin Luther King, the apostle of&nbsp;
non-violence and the&nbsp; Civil Rights movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.880
has been shot to death in Memphis Tennessee.
..And you have to think that he knows that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:11.360
knows what he has in the trunk of his car,&nbsp;
that he has a film that is already whether,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:18.560
you know, he overtly understands it or&nbsp;
claims it is a brilliant piece of art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.840
but also human and political history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.800
It is notable that when the&nbsp;
movie was re-released in 1969,&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:32.320
it was advertised as a double-feature alongside&nbsp;
other movies that were explicitly about race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:33.120
like Slaves.&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:37.440
Despite Romero’s denial of any&nbsp;
intentional racial allegories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.000
Duane Jones appears to have&nbsp;
had a different perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:45.280
The original script described the character&nbsp;
of Ben as a white, redneck truck driver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:50.720
But Jones insisted that the character’s speech&nbsp;
and wardrobe should be more sophisticated.&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.680
Romero even said that Jones “didn’t want to&nbsp;
look gruff and he wanted to be presentable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:59.280
He was the one who was much more worried&nbsp;
about any of that then we were.”&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.800
Finally, having heard Night of the Living Dead&nbsp;
called a political film time and time again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:10.880
Romero seemingly relented. He acknowledged that he&nbsp;
may have unconsciously had certain themes in mind.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:17.600
This is an important, powerful text if for&nbsp;
no other reason that it emerges right in the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:26.240
in the sort of context of King's assassination&nbsp;
and says, you will see, and appreciate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:32.400
the power, the resonance of blackness. That's&nbsp;
what makes Night of the Living Dead so special.&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.252
George has great affection for his human characters, but he essentially saw them as doomed

00:10:38.252 --> 00:10:46.512
and maybe as the human race was sort of deservedly doomed and that we had to make way for this uprising

00:10:46.512 --> 00:10:51.260
So it was always intended as an uprising narrative. 

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:56.560
Romero’s next zombie movie, Dawn of the&nbsp;
Dead, came out in 1978, and portrayed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:01.600
same type of zombie, but moved the setting to a&nbsp;
shopping mall—then a growing site of importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.400
in American consumer culture and a symbol of&nbsp;
mass culture and homogenous suburban life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:13.360
The movie opens in a newsroom where the media&nbsp;
becomes the target of Romero’s social critique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.440
The news anchor says he does not believe&nbsp;
the doctor being interviewed despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:21.600
professional’s insistence that the dead&nbsp;
have risen and begun attacking the living.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:27.200
In a downtown setting SWAT team officers&nbsp;
gun down housing project residents seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:31.920
indiscriminately before a group&nbsp;
of survivors flees the city in a helicopter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:36.160
They seek shelter in a large shopping&nbsp;
center where zombies roam outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:39.040
drawn by some memory of the&nbsp;
place having significance.&nbsp;

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:46.400
The humor and ridiculousness of some of&nbsp;
the scenes in Dawn of the Dead reflect the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:50.880
comedic over- exaggeration and gore&nbsp;
endemic to late 70s horror movies.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:12:03.760
Dawn spawned this entire genre that was bigger and&nbsp;
brighter, and had these elements of joy in it that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:10.160
were missing from the earlier zombie works,&nbsp;
so I think it just was the perfect kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.120
film at the perfect time in&nbsp;
the seventies. That really just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.400
is the kind of thing that&nbsp;
makes fan boys and fan girls.

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:24.480
&nbsp;It made him to the godfather of the zombies.&nbsp;

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:31.200
Dawn of the Dead was followed by Day of the Dead&nbsp;
in 1985, in which science takes a front-seat as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:37.840
military personnel and scientists attempt to stop&nbsp;
or reverse zombification in an underground bunker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:45.760
Of course, there’s a doctor performing strange&nbsp;
experiments on the undead—another trope Romero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:50.480
helped to establish in the zombie genre.
Interestingly, Romero would later say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:55.520
that Day of the Dead was unique for its use of&nbsp;
the zombie Bub as the film’s main character.&nbsp;

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:00.800
Bub defies zombie norms by showing&nbsp;
some level of intelligence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:06.960
since he is trained to not act violently.
He’s friendly to most of the living characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:12.800
and remembers some parts of his previous life.&nbsp;
He even speaks and shoots a gun at one point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:18.400
Bub marks the first, but not the last,&nbsp;
of Romero’s zombies who appear capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:25.360
domestication. Romero is humanizing the undead.&nbsp;
A sign that the zombie was changing, both in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:31.040
meaning and popularity, was clearly demonstrated&nbsp;
in the 1990 Night of the Living Dead remake.&nbsp;

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:36.880
Romero rewrote the original script&nbsp;
himself, which featured key changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:40.880
like making Barbara actually capable&nbsp;
of contributing to her own survival.&nbsp;

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.160
The establishment of stronger female&nbsp;
characters in horror and action movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:53.360
since the 1970s and the feminist movement can&nbsp;
account for Barbara’s altered character arc.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.000 --> 00:14:00.800
Romero even said that 90’s Barbara was “his&nbsp;
apology to women” for the 1968 original character.&nbsp;

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:09.040
Ben is still killed, but only&nbsp;
after he has become a zombie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.720
justifying his death, and seems to be an&nbsp;
attempt to remove any racial commentary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:18.960
The dramatic cultural and social differences&nbsp;
between the late 20th and early 21st centuries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:24.880
are also reflected in the 2004 remake of&nbsp;
Dawn of the Dead. Like all Romero films, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:30.720
“reimagining” as the creators like to call it, is&nbsp;
profoundly reflective of its place in history.&nbsp;

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:35.760
While still set in a mall, the opening scenes&nbsp;
of the zombie attacks and instant apocalyptic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:41.360
chaos of the undead is jarring to Americans. -&nbsp;
And let’s be honest, those scenes of the undead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:45.360
swarming the mall and banging on the&nbsp;
doors read differently once you’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:51.440
those videos of how people can get&nbsp;
2005 saw the release of Land of the Dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:56.240
set in Pittsburgh where the safety of the&nbsp;
living is ensured by a feudal-like government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:02.000
Romero’s application of the zombie film as&nbsp;
social commentary is again on full display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:07.280
as the survivors with money and power live in a&nbsp;
luxury apartment building while the majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:13.600
the population lives in slums. Oh, and suburbia?&nbsp;
Yeah, that’s been completely overrun by zombies.&nbsp;

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.720
For me this film stands out because&nbsp;
of the character of Big Daddy—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:25.120
Black zombie hero with intelligence, who leads&nbsp;
the other zombies and uses tools. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:30.400
figures out that zombies can walk underwater&nbsp;
and use machine guns, so that’s terrifying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:35.920
The movie ends with Big Daddy and a group of&nbsp;
his undead followers leaving the city and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:42.240
attacking the living, which to me reads as another&nbsp;
homage to I Am Legend as well as Romero again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:47.520
alluding to the ascension of a new generation&nbsp;
that uses ingenuity and makes the best out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:53.120
bad circumstances. Perhaps this is also an&nbsp;
intentional contrast with the violent end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:58.640
the Black character in Night of the Living Dead.
In 2007, Diary of the Dead introduced us to&nbsp; a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:03.600
group of college film students navigating the&nbsp;
zombie apocalypse with the aid of their only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:09.760
source of up-to-date information—bloggers on the&nbsp;
Internet. This penultimate Romero zombie film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:15.120
explored analogies including our eroding social&nbsp;
connections, the effect of emerging technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:20.400
on our bodies and our brains, and the collapse&nbsp;
of civilization in a post-apocalyptic setting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:26.960
Indeed, as technology advances, it is increasingly&nbsp;
presented as threatening to our very humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:30.560
and even in some cases, the&nbsp;
direct cause of a zombie plague&nbsp;

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:34.160
Romero definitely wanted to make this&nbsp;
connection in Diary of the Dead.&nbsp;

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:39.200
In an interview, he said that the film was in&nbsp;
part inspired by his concern of the explosion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:43.760
“alternate media, the blogosphere and all that,&nbsp;
and it just occurred to me that there’s some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:48.800
dangers lying here potentially hidden. It just&nbsp;
really struck me that this was what was going on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:54.880
in the world now, everyone’s a camera, everyone’s&nbsp;
a reporter, and people seem to be obsessed by it.”&nbsp;

00:16:54.880 --> 00:17:00.320
The last zombie film Romero wrote and directed,&nbsp;
Survival of the Dead, premiered in 2009&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:05.440
and focuses on two families whose mutual&nbsp;
hostility and loathing -- diseases of the mind and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:11.200
soul --&nbsp; long predate the arrival of the&nbsp; zombie&nbsp;
infection. The movie culminates in a gruesome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:17.520
scene in which the two reanimated patriarchs&nbsp;
attempt to kill each other—again. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:22.400
the zombies are a backdrop, a foil for festering&nbsp;
social, personal, and political conflict.&nbsp;

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:26.160
I think that one of the reasons zombies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:33.280
become so popular in recent years is again&nbsp;
the kind of a retread of that fear of social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:40.160
upheaval that we saw in the 1960s. You only have&nbsp;
to open a newspaper or watch a news program today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:46.560
to see the fears represented by the segment&nbsp;
of the population that is afraid of change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:51.680
That doesn't like the way the nation looks,&nbsp;
because it doesn't look as white as they thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:56.480
it's supposed to look. And that segment of the&nbsp;
population that's afraid because they're being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:02.640
targeted because of their race and, and, and in&nbsp;
policing and in mass incarceration. So I think all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:08.240
of those fears are kind of at a, at a boil.&nbsp;
That’s what Romero’s real legacy is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:13.120
he created a monster whose genesis is&nbsp;
vague and whose manifestation is rot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:18.800
and juxtaposed it with American society at the&nbsp;
turn of the 21st century. His zombies have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:24.240
from the beginning, represented so much more&nbsp;
than just mindless flesh-eating automatons.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:28.560
He had done night and Dawn and&nbsp;
that alone was going to make him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:35.600
a legend. And it didn't hurt that he was, you&nbsp;
know, 11 feet tall and wore giant black glasses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:42.000
and was just incredibly amiable. Um, he, he&nbsp;
was a perfect Emissary for horror, and the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:46.560
that the people who created can be extremely&nbsp;
approachable and gentle and kind Ramiro was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:51.760
pacifist. He might've made these violent movies,&nbsp;
but they were all in aid of his, his beliefs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:56.320
and his politics, which were largely, you&nbsp;
know, learn how to walk away from a fight.

00:18:56.320 --> 00:19:00.400
In a 2010 interview Romero said&nbsp;
he largely thinks his movies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:06.240
“overanalyzed” and he’s “not into it.” But, I&nbsp;
don’t&nbsp; think he’s giving himself enough credit.&nbsp;

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:11.600
Regardless of the original intentions of the&nbsp;
filmmaker, ultimately the audience gets to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:17.200
decide what it means. Our own experiences&nbsp;
and perspectives influence how we interpret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:21.920
any kind of art, including zombie films.&nbsp;
So to end, I’ll use the late,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:27.440
great horror icon’s own words: “All the&nbsp;
monsters we’ve created in fiction, unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:34.160
expressly identified as the Devil, represent our&nbsp;
own evil. We create them so we can kill them off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:39.360
thereby justifying ourselves—it’s&nbsp;
a kind of penance, self-exorcism.”

